From fecc64178e824b8b25a2c795ed4b8ab568d49d8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wooseong Kim <innocarpe@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:12:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(pager): report explicit OSC 9999 agent status to Orca
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Orca reads an explicit status stream out of the pane's PTY instead of
guessing a turn boundary from the tab title. dsb never sent one, so an
Orca pane showed no status at all: the sidebar row, the mobile session
row, and the turn-complete notification all stayed empty.

The tab title cannot close that gap. Orca's agent vocabulary is a closed
set with no dsb member, so a dsb title is either ignored or misread —
`DeepSeek Build` resolves to no agent, and the braille spinner makes a
working pane read as a working Claude. Measured against Orca 1.4.210:
`tui-idle` times out on a real dsb pane, and a hand-sent OSC 9999 frame
with `agentType: "deepseek-build"` lands in `orca worktree ps` as
`state: working` → `done`.

Emit the frame from the same ticks that repaint the title so the two can
never disagree about the turn state, and close it out on shutdown and on
both idle edges — tick demand falls to None once a pane rests, so the
closing frame cannot rely on a later tick.

Gated on the host: the sequence means nothing outside a host that parses
it, so it stays silent everywhere else rather than appending bytes to
every terminal dsb runs in. Deduped per frame so a resting pane does not
rewrite an identical sequence several times a second.

This is the status layer only. Detection and launch still need an Orca
change: identity comes from a host-side vocabulary this patch cannot
extend, and a frame naming an unknown agent establishes no identity.
